ent_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5311;}i:519;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1:" ";}i:2;i:5315;}i:520;a:3:{i:0;s:11:"strong_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5316;}i:521;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:7:"Kurrung";}i:2;i:5318;}i:522;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"strong_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5325;}i:523;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:426:": Sep-Oct, the hot, dry time where the air is fragrant from many plants flowering, like Manboyberre (Syzgium forte) and Mandjarduk (Syzgium suborbiculare). It’s a very good time for hunting waterbirds like Manimunak (magpie goose), Ngalmangiyi (northern long-necked turtle) with kawurluwurlhme kunak (floodplain burning) to clear the kundalk (grass) as well as hunt Kedjebe (Arafura filesnake) around the edge of billabongs.";}i:2;i:5327;}i:524;a:3:{i:0;s:17:"listcontent_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5753;}i:525;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"listitem_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5753;}i:526;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"listitem_open";i:1;a:1:{i:0;i:1;}i:2;i:5753;}i:527;a:3:{i:0;s:16:"listcontent_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5753;}i:528;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1:" ";}i:2;i:5757;}i:529;a:3:{i:0;s:11:"strong_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5758;}i:530;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:10:"Kunumeleng";}i:2;i:5760;}i:531;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"strong_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5770;}i:532;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:353:": Nov-Dec, the time of kangolmarnburren, when the humidity builds, kunngol (clouds) and kunmayorrk (wind) gather together from all directions. Animals hear kangurdulme (thunder) and know it’s time for a change. Namarrkon kamayhmayhke (lightning flashes) tell us that the Ngalmangiyi and Kedjebe are moving closer to the bank and are easier to collect.";}i:2;i:5772;}i:533;a:3:{i:0;s:17:"listcontent_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6125;}i:534;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"listitem_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6125;}i:535;a:3:{i:0;s:11:"listo_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6125;}i:536;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"section_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6127;}i:537;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"header";i:1;a:3:{i:0;s:39:"Cultural and Environmental Significance";i:1;i:2;i:2;i:6127;}i:2;i:6127;}i:538;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"section_open";i:1;a:1:{i:0;i:2;}i:2;i:6127;}i:539;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6127;}i:540;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:209:"There is a deep connection between people, land, and animals. Knowledge about the seasons has been passed down through generations. It helps manage natural resources, predict weather, and sustain biodiversity.";}i:2;i:6180;}i:541;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6389;}i:542;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"section_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6391;}i:543;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"document_end";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6391;}}